<h2>The proceeding under challenge</h2>
<p>The parties married on 2 March 2017 and have a son. The wife's complaint became Bagnan P.S. Case No. 431 of 2020, alleging cruelty by the husband and his relatives; a charge sheet followed against three accused, and the case was pending as G.R. Case No. 2275 of 2020. The husband — who said he was studying in Haridwar during the relevant period — moved the High Court under Section 482 CrPC read with Article 227 (the jurisdiction now carried by Section 528 BNSS) to quash the proceeding.</p>

<h2>The Court's analysis</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Vague allegations</strong> — The claims of physical and mental torture were bald, with no dates, times or manner disclosed (para 26).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>No statutory cruelty</strong> — Not answering phone calls cannot drive a woman to suicide or cause grave injury; Explanation (a) to Section 498A was not met (para 28).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>No demand alleged</strong> — With no dowry demand pleaded, Explanation (b) had no application either (para 29).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Section 34 baseless</strong> — No common intention was alleged, so the aid of Section 34 was unavailable (para 31).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Bhajan Lal categories</strong> — The case fell within categories (1) and (3); continuing against the husband alone, after the in-laws' exoneration, would abuse the process (para 35). The proceeding was quashed qua the petitioner (paras 36-37).</div>
</div>

<p>Justice Ajay Kumar Gupta also disposed of two recurring side-arguments. The <em>Lalita Kumari</em> point — that the FIR was vitiated for want of a preliminary inquiry — failed, because the discretion to hold such an inquiry in matrimonial cases is not a precondition to registration (para 32). And defences touching the parallel Domestic Violence Act case were left to be decided in those proceedings (para 34).</p>

<h2>A pattern worth noting</h2>
<p>This is the second 498A quashing delivered by the same Bench on the same day — in the companion decision, a prosecution built on an alleged extramarital affair met the same fate. Read together, the orders restate a consistent judicial method: identify the specific acts alleged, test them against the Explanation to Section 498A, and refuse to let adjectives substitute for particulars.</p>

<table class="law">
<tr><th>Allegation type</th><th>Survives quashing scrutiny?</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dated, specific acts of violence or harassment linked to demands</td><td>Yes — matter for trial</td></tr>
<tr><td>General words — "torture", "cruelty" — without particulars</td><td>No — Bhajan Lal categories (1) and (3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conduct outside the Explanation (unanswered calls, estrangement)</td><td>No — not the statutory offence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Allegations already rejected qua co-accused, repeated verbatim</td><td>Weakened — abuse of process analysis applies</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For practitioners:</strong> where co-accused family members have already been exonerated, obtain those orders and plead them: they frame the surviving prosecution's weakness. For complainants, the same orders are a drafting lesson — a complaint that records specific incidents with dates and context survives; one that relies on conclusory labels may not reach trial at all.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Quashing under the inherent power is confined to the criminal case. The matrimonial relationship, maintenance questions and the pending DV Act proceeding remain to be worked out in their own forums — on evidence, not adjectives.</p>
</div>
